{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15061 - PROTEIN:5677",
  "verbalization": "Chaxamycin B appears to exert its biological effects through a series of interconnected molecular pathways that ultimately target JM1. The drug is directly linked to the enzyme CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which is a key player in the synthesis of sialic acid. This enzyme is also a target of 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide and (1S,3E,4S,5S,6S)-4,5-dihydroxy-3-(3-methylbut-2-enylidene)-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptan-2-one, both of which are implicated in the regulation of JM1. The presence of multiple targets suggests a complex, multi-step mechanism of action, where Chaxamycin B may influence cellular processes through the modulation of sialic acid synthesis and subsequent downstream effects on JM1. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust and reliable relationship, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
